  "summary": "After a RoundTable inquiry, Flock Safety removed the last two license plate cameras it had left installed in Evanston six months after the city terminated its contract in August 2025 over the company's data sharing with federal agencies including CBP. FOIA-obtained emails reveal Flock's CEO called EPD Chief Schenita Stewart to apologize and the company floated a 'proposal' to continue the relationship, which the city declined; the contract dispute remains unresolved with no 2026 payment made.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Despite the City of Evanston ordering Flock Safety to take down all of its automated license plate readers last August, the company left two of the cameras installed and covered for the last six months, before swiftly removing them this week after the RoundTable requested comment on their presence.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Following reports last summer that the company shared vehicle data with federal law enforcement agencies, including U.S. Customs and Border Protection, the city moved in August to terminate the contract early and remove the 18 stationary cameras",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "our CEO, Garrett [Langley], is going to place a call to the Chief to apologize directly for the events of the past couple of months",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "the 'proposal' concerned Flock making an offer to 'continue the relationship,' which the city declined",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    }
  ],
